**Abstract.** What the walkthroughs found means for the Use Cases, how each finding feeds back into the Foundation, the Specification and the planned studies, the limits of the walkthroughs and studies, and what the next iteration does. Morning care has a full walkthrough (ER-01). Kinnect had a peer walkthrough of its prototype in the lab session of 13 October (ER-04), with verbal findings only.

= Interpretation of the walkthroughs =

== Morning care (ER-01) ==

ER-01 walked UC04 as Mary and Myra. The prompting ladder itself held up: at SA-05 steps 4 to 7, where Mary has started and faces the cue, the wait and the L1 and L2 prompts gave credible success stories, and so did the early-stop offer (EV-01, EV-06). The failures sit at the edges of the ladder. Before it starts, a resident who stands at the sink without lifting anything gets no support (WF-01). When it ends in a handover, Mary hears nothing for up to 10 minutes (WF-06), and Myra could not tell which room to go to (WF-07) or how to acknowledge (WF-08). After the session, the record does not tell Myra at which step Mary needed help (WF-09). In short, UC04 specifies the prompting well and the transitions into and out of it poorly.

This shapes ER-02. A rise in distress in B (UC04-F8-CL13) could come from the wait W, which the Claim names, or from these transitions. ER-02 therefore codes M-14 per step and logs which ladder rule fired, so that a rise can be traced to its step. UC04-F9-CL15 was challenged on the Spec-1 handover; ER-02 tests it on the revised steps 9 and 10, which first need walking again.

== Kinnect (ER-04) ==

The peers' main point was that the prototype took too long to get to an introduction: it asked many personal questions and rarely suggested a match (KF-02). UC02-F3-CL1 rests on introductions happening, so this qualifies it (EV-08). The specification already places profile building with the coordinator (UC01 step 2), so the finding mostly concerns Build 1, which let the robot build the profile in conversation. It also concerns ER-03, whose intervention spends week 1 on one-to-one profile sessions before any matching. The second point was the robot itself: Pepper felt too clunky to take on a walk with others, and the peers preferred a small worn device that could also notice incidents (KF-03). That questions the choice of Pepper for the walking part of UC02 and asks for an alternative the Foundation does not yet compare. A worn device that notices incidents is close to the wearable location sensing that TECH-09 rejects as surveillance, so the peers' preference and VT-06 pull in opposite directions.

